    {/* ---------- AT THE TABLE ---------- */}
    <section class="block" id="table">
      <p class="section-label">At the table</p>
      <h2 class="section-h2">For the data nerds: your log is files.</h2>
      <p class="prose">The phone is where the set gets written down. What you do with the record after that is up to you: it leaves the app as plain files, in a published format, whenever you ask, and any tool you like can read them.</p>
      <div class="ladder">
        <div class="rung"><span class="n">1</span><div><h3>The log</h3><p class="what">An append-only file per day, one event per set, that the app writes and nothing rewrites. Corrections are new lines, never edits. <em>Export the raw log</em> in Settings hands it over as it is, one line per event.</p></div></div>
        <div class="rung"><span class="n">2</span><div><h3>The export</h3><p class="what"><em>Export all my data</em> writes one file in the Mission Built Schema: every workout and set, your programs, saved workouts and exercises, and your units. Map coordinates only if you switch them on. Anything that reads JSON reads it, and <em>Bring in data</em> takes it back on a new phone.</p></div></div>
        <div class="rung"><span class="n">3</span><div><h3>Any tool</h3><p class="what">A spreadsheet, a script, a notebook, a database, or the dashboards you already run. I index mine into Elasticsearch and read it in Kibana: how heavy a week was, whether I am ramping, what I have been neglecting. That is a choice, not a requirement; the file works the same in whatever you use.</p></div></div>
      </div>
